    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3><p>Given that \(\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dx} = {@constant@}\), we need to find the function \(f(x)\), where \(y = f(x)\).</p>

<p>The differential equation \(\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dx} = {@constant@}\) indicates that the derivative of \(y\) with respect to \(x\) is a constant, specifically \({@constant@}\). To find \(y\), we need to integrate this derivative.</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dx} = {@constant@}\)</p>

<p>Integrating both sides with respect to \(x\) gives:</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle y = \int {@constant@} \, dx\)</p>

<p>The integral of \({@constant@}\) with respect to \(x\) is:</p>

<p>\(y = {@ta@}\)<p>

<p>where \(c\) is the constant of integration.</p>

<p>Therefore, the function \(f(x)\) is:</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle f(x) = {@ta@}\)</p>

<p>This represents the general solution to the given differential equation. The constant \(C\) can take any real value, depending on the initial conditions or additional information provided about the problem.</p>]]></text>
